  "query_preview": "/Through Saturday/...\nIssued at 1222 PM MST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery shows a trough over Colorado with a strong upper \nlevel jet across the eastern half of the state. This jet and PVA \nfrom the trough are providing forcing for what has turned into a \ndecent snow event for the foothills and western suburbs of the \nDenver metro. There were some reports of 3-5\" of snow in the \nfoothills with up to 2\" in the western metro. This jet is still \nproducing snow showers mainly in two areas. One area is in Morgan \nand eastern Weld Counties where the best low level frontogenesis in \noccurring. With the little bit of added elevation in northeastern\nWeld County, some snow accumulation may have occurred and may \ncontinue this afternoon. The other area of snow is mainly across \nthe southern foothills and South Park. It's possible some areas \nnear Westcreek receive 1-3\" of snow from the snow showers this \nafternoon mainly on grassy surfaces. PoPs were increased for both \nof those areas this afternoon. In the Denver metro, there appears \nto be too much dry air for any snow showers to form this \nafternoon. Otherwise, there will be isolated convective snow \nshowers this afternoon over the higher terrain as some instability\nbuilds in. \n\nTonight, skies will clear as the trough moves past Colorado. Light \nnortheast winds will advect moisture from the Northern Plains into \nColorado. There may be some patchy fog that develops and with \ntemperatures below freezing, it could create some slick spots on the \nroadways. \n\nOn Sunday, weak ridging will be overhead which will keep conditions \ndry. Temperatures will remain cool with high clouds and generally \nlight winds. \n\nOn Monday, a shortwave trough will move over Colorado. The main PVA \nforcing from this trough will be split around Colorado with a \nwave over New Mexico and another in Montana. Lee cyclogenesis \nwill develop across northeast Colorado which will attempt to bring\nin moisture but there will remain very limited moisture with this\nsystem. Due to the weak forcing and limited moisture, the plains \nare expected to remain dry. The I-70 corridor mountains, Park \nCounty, and the southern foothills will have enough instability \nthat develops that snow showers will likely (70% chance) form. \nDespite the high chance of snow, the snow amounts will be \nminimal. Generally less than 2\" of snow is expected in the \nmountains with light impacts. \n\nThe back edge of the trough will move east of Colorado Tuesday \nafternoon. Most ensemble members are showing ridging generally over \nColorado and the Southwest US starting Wednesday and lasting \nthrough the weekend. However, a few (roughly 10-20%) have a trough\nmoving close to Colorado out of northwest flow that could produce\nlight precipitation mainly in the mountains of Colorado Thursday \ninto Friday. Therefore, the forecast calls for a dry Thanksgiving \nin Denver and across the plains with a chance of light snow in the\nmountains. Overall, no major travel impacts are expected for the \nThanksgiving travel period.",
